  "statements": {
    "point-of-application": "Where a force is applied decides whether it rotates a body, so a zero net external force is necessary for equilibrium but not sufficient. Two equal and opposite forces along the same line leave an ice hockey stick motionless, while the same two forces applied at different places set it rotating.",
    "balanced-seesaw": "To balance a seesaw, take the torques about its pivot, which makes the torque of the supporting force zero, and set the two children's torques equal and opposite. The heavier child must then sit closer to the pivot, at $r_2 = r_1 m_1 / m_2$, and the first condition gives the supporting force as the sum of the two weights.",
    "restoring-torque": "The torque a body's own weight produces once it is displaced decides its kind of equilibrium. If that torque turns the body back toward where it was, the equilibrium is stable; if it turns the body further away, the equilibrium is unstable; and if there is none, the equilibrium is neutral.",
    "joint-force": "Once the muscle force is known, the first condition for equilibrium gives the force in the joint, and it is large too. A muscle can contract but cannot push, so the muscle and the joint pull in opposite directions and their forces subtract to leave the weight actually supported.",
    "benefits-of-short-lever-arms": "Muscles are attached close to the joints they move because a short lever arm lets a small contraction swing the limb through a large distance in a short time. The arrangement pays in force for speed, and it also leaves room for many joints and the wide ranges over which they work."
  },
